 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049953 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Spheres" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049953 #edb/ref 1033 1146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049953 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "20ec2a7d-e711-5ee0-a76f-c2c6805b5674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1031 "Surface Areas of Spheres" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1033 1765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050966 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76391ee0-ed5a-5ed3-8cef-c09827219e58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119719 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119719 #edb/ref 1071 "A sphere has a diameter of $3 \\: \\textrm{in}$. Find the surface area of the sphere." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119719 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fba82316-1ea7-5c90-88d7-37b96172a7f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119719 #edb/ref 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\: \\textrm{in}\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi \\left(\\dfrac {3} {2} \\right)^2 = 9\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119720 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119720 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the radius of a sphere given that its surface area is $50\\pi \\: \\textrm{cm}^2$.\n\n![](images/99/99bb0ea67859fbd109442fcf698427ea43452087b4523b7202a760a2a8b27052.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119720 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e65da51f-3cfc-511a-948c-ba8a46559bba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119720 #edb/ref 1074 "We can calculate the surface area of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 4 \\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nS=50\\pi \\: \\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n50π &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{50π}{4π} &= r^{2} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{25}{2} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119721 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119721 #edb/ref 1071 "The volume of the sphere is $\\dfrac{500}{3}\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3$. Calculate the surface area of the sphere." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acdb3e99-89dd-5463-871e-e4d5d370c827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119721 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the volume of a sphere using the formula\n\n$$\nV = \\dfrac 4 3 \\pi r^3\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius of the sphere.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nV=\\dfrac{500}{3}\\pi \\: \\textrm{in}^3\n$$\n\ninto the formula, we can solve for $r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{500}{3}π &= \\frac{4}{3}πr^{3} \\\\\n\\frac{500π \\cdot 3}{3 \\cdot 4π} &= r^{3} \\\\\nr^{3} &= 125 \\\\\nr &= 5in\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of ​​the sphere is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 4πr^{2} \\\\\n&= 4π(5)^{2} \\\\\n&= 100πin^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119971 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Surface Area of a Sphere Given Its R